course: dictionary
lesson: 2260
title: Creak: a sound you can hear
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like the thing they describe. Creak is one of them: say it slowly and it sounds a bit like an old door opening. Writers use words like this to help you hear a story, and they are called sound words.
explain: «She lay awake till three, listening to every creak.» Here creak is a noun, which means a thing. It is the sound itself. The words lay awake and listening tell you the room is quiet and she is nervous, so a creak is a small sound that is easy to hear in the dark.
explain: Creak can also be a verb, a word for doing something: «The stairs creaked as someone walked up them.» Creak can also mean something that is failing: «The old system is creaking» means it is working badly because it is old or weak.
fact: To creak is to make a long, high, squeaking sound, like an old door, a wooden floor or stairs when they move or are walked on.
fact: A creak is that sound: «Houses creak as they cool down at night or when the wind blows; creaks are not ghosts.»
fact: Night-time creaks usually come from mice, branches, wind, cooling pipes or a house settling, which means its wood and walls move a tiny bit.
fact: Writers use creaking doors and floorboards to build suspense, the feeling that something is about to happen.
fact: Oiling hinges stops them creaking, so a squeaky door can be fixed.
exercise: «The floorboards gave a loud creak under her feet.» In this sentence, what is a creak? | A kind of shoe | A bright light | *A long, high, squeaking sound | A pet mouse
exercise: «The old system is creaking.» What does creaking mean here? | *Working badly because it is old or weak | Making a squeaking sound like a door | Running very fast | Shining brightly
exercise: Which word fits the gap? «The old gate ___ open at midnight.» | laughed | sparkled | tasted | *creaked
test: «She lay awake, listening to every creak.» Where do night-time creaks usually come from? | *Mice, branches, wind or a house settling | A monster under the bed | Gremlins in the attic | Ghosts looking for a home
test: Which sentence uses creak as a noun, meaning the sound itself? | *«The floor gave a loud creak.» | «The floor will creak if you tiptoe.» | «The floor creaked under my feet.»
exam: Why are creaks in a spooky story usually not ghosts? | Because ghosts never make any sound | *Because houses can creak as they cool down at night or when the wind blows | Because creaks only happen in the morning | Because stories are always true
